{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob08re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Luke</strong>: Since programming can be too demanding, sometimes, you may need to use strategic statements to reduce the amount of work.<br/>\n<strong>Ivette</strong>: I've done programming, but I quitted. At a point it got really tiring to work over something endlessly.<br/>\n<strong>Luke</strong>: I know what you mean. There will be times where you need to perform actions over and over again. For such tasks, loops really ease down the burden of writing the same piece of code multiple times.<br/>\n<strong>Ivette</strong>: Maybe you could tell me more about those loops.", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob08re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Luke</strong>: Sure, there are multiple types of loops in JavaScript, the first two are the most common ones, and they are similar. The first one, the \"while\" loop, is used to execute a statement or code block repeatedly as long as the expression is true. Once it becomes false, the loop finishes and moves to another command.<br/>\n<strong>Ivette</strong>: And what about the syntax in this loop?<br/>\n<strong>Luke</strong>: Do not forget to use the \"while\" keyword and do not omit breaks. I know it can be tiring to check twice before launching, but it's more difficult to find a misspelled code once it's been finished.<br/>\n<strong>Ivette</strong>: You're right! You may feel more satisfied by being extra careful when writing in JavaScript. Let's talk about the next loop statement.", "time": "32000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob08re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Luke</strong>: The \"do...while\" loop is similar to the \"while\" loop but in this case, the command is executed at least once no matter if the condition is true or false. For this, do not forget to use a semicolon at the end of the loop. And please, do not forget that these loops are different. They may seem similar at the beginning, but having problems with the code because of the wrong loop can be very frustrating. <br/>\n<strong>Ivette</strong>: I'm sure people might be interested to hear more about loops, let's go to a break before we continue. Stay tuned.", "time": "82000"}]}}], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can use while loop statements in JavaScript.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about the purpose of a while loop script.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify the syntax of while loop statements in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What are the advantages of JavaScript compared to other programming languages?  ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Why has JavaScript become so popular among programmers? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "How to Make Repetition Easier in JavaScript", "type": "free_type_text_area"}]}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "STI", "stimuli_ids": [1], "questions": [{"id": 0, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "